  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1207 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nToday - Thursday: Widespread Rain\n\nThe forecast remains largely on track for widespread rain today as \nnear maximum NAEFS percentile PWATs (1.4 to 1.7\") push northward \ninto the region and combine with lift from a shortwave trough\nand accompanying surface low developing in the Northern Plains.\nInitial rainfall arrives locally in western peripheral counties\nof southeast Minnesota by mid-morning, becoming more widespread\nby late morning. Rainfall persists into Tuesday as another wave\npushes north, reinvigorating low-level flow and accompanying\nsurge of moisture. The latest trends, however, that are \nsupported by the ECMWF and GFS and their respective ensemble and\nAI ensemble models, continue to show that this moisture surge \njust misses most of the local forecast area, with the exception\nof portions of northeast Iowa and far southwest Wisconsin. What\nthis means is that while rain will continue through the day on \nTuesday, the heaviest amounts stay to our south. \n\nAnother forecast trend is decreasing rainfall amounts.\nConvective allowing and global models agree that the bulk of \nthe moisture remains northwest of the local forecast area\nMonday, and northwest/south for Tuesday. As a result, the \nprobabilities for 1\" of rain continue to decrease. Currently \nthe best chance for seeing at least 1\" of rain from this \nmorning through Wednesday morning are for areas south of I-90 \nand areas north of I-94 (40 to 70%). Within these areas, total \nrain amounts are generally in the 0.75 to 1.25\" ranges and \noutside of these locations it is 0.5 to 0.75\". While there are still\nsome ensemble members that have higher totals closer to 1-2\", \nhigher outliers have continued to decrease over the past couple\nof days. \n\nMore rain chances (30 to 60%) Wednesday and Thursday are from\nthe upper-level low situated over the region. Temperatures for \ntoday and Tuesday remain in the mid 60s with near record warm  \nlow temperatures for tonight with temperatures in the mid 50s to\neven some low 60s. Due to the cloud coverage, ongoing rain, and\npersistent warm air advection, temperatures for tonight will \nnot differ that much from the high temperatures for today or \nTuesday. Current forecasted low temperatures at KLSE and KRST \nare 61 degrees and 59 degrees respectively. Both these \ntemperatures would not only be daily warm low records but they \nwould also be the monthly warm low record for both locations. \n\nLate Week: Trending Drier with Above Normal Temperatures\n\nZonal flow takes over through Saturday before another wave dips down \ninto the Upper Midwest. Current cluster analyses show 80% of \nensemble solutions supporting a troughing solution, differing \nby location and magnitude, while the other 20% suggests\ntroughing farther south through the southern Plains. Therefore,\nsubsequent local rainfall chances across the region after \nThursday. With the area under zonal flow, high temperatures look\nto remain above normal with highs generally in the low to mid \n50s and lows in the mid 30s to low 40s.",
